From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Owner Placeholder <owner@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 20:17:58 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] powerpc/rust: build Rust code in modules with the large code
 model

C code in modules is built with -mcmodel=large: with the medium model
the compiler forms the address of data that it may assume to be within
2 GB of the TOC with a TOC-relative addis/addi pair, and in a module
that assumption does not hold. The per-cpu section is moved into the
kernel's per-cpu area (0xc...), far from the module's TOC, and since
module text, data and rodata are separate vmalloc allocations nothing
keeps the rest within 2 GB either. module_64.c does not range-check
R_PPC64_TOC16_HA and R_PPC64_TOC16_LO, so such an access is silently
relocated to the wrong address instead of failing the load.

rustc is not given a code model on powerpc, so Rust code in modules is
built with LLVM's default for ppc64, which is medium. Pass
-Ccode-model=large for modules, as for C. Built-in Rust code keeps the
default, like built-in C code.

Signed-off-by: OWNER NAME <owner@example.invalid>
---
 arch/powerpc/Makefile | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/powerpc/Makefile b/arch/powerpc/Makefile
index 9385db478..733e4f872 100644
--- a/arch/powerpc/Makefile
+++ b/arch/powerpc/Makefile
@@ -123,6 +123,8 @@ ifndef CONFIG_PPC_KERNEL_PCREL
 	# kernel percpu data space (starting with 0xc...). We need a full
 	# 64bit relocation for this to work, hence -mcmodel=large.
 	KBUILD_CFLAGS_MODULE += -mcmodel=large
+	# The same holds for Rust code in modules.
+	KBUILD_RUSTFLAGS_MODULE += -Ccode-model=large
 endif
 endif
 
